counterexample:
-- No INSERT statements can cause a difference
-- because Q1 and Q2 are semantically equivalent under standard SQL.

-- For example, the following INSERTs will yield identical results in both queries:
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (1, 'a');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (2, 'a');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (3, 'b');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (4, 'b');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (5, 'c');

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2019-12-31', 10, 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 11, 101);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-05', 20, 200);

-- Under the current schema, no test data can produce different results for Q1 and Q2 because the PK on (c4, c2) guarantees that the minimum c2 is unique per c4.
-- To illustrate the potential difference (which is only possible if the PK were removed), one might have inserted the following rows (this would violate the PK constraint and is given here for illustration only):

-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 100, 0);
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 200, 0);

-- Since such duplicate rows are disallowed by the declared schema, any counterexample based on duplicate minimum dates is impossible. Therefore, with the given schema the two queries are equivalent.

