counterexample:
-- Under the declared schema (c2 is unique and c1 is Boolean), every test database yields Q1 and Q2 with the same output. For example, consider the following data:

INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (2, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (4, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (5, 1);

Here Q1 works as follows:
  • For c2 = 1, the right neighbor (c2 = 2) is valid so the row qualifies.
  • For c2 = 2, the left neighbor (c2 = 1) qualifies so it qualifies.
  • For c2 = 4 and 5, they form a consecutive block (neighbors 4 and 5 exist) so they qualify if compared among themselves, while a gap between 2 and 4 means that the row with c2 = 2 is not considered adjacent to the ones in the block {4,5}.
Thus Q1 returns {1,2} from the first block and {4,5} from the second block (only blocks of at least 2 are kept).
Q2, by definition, selects rows from table1 with c1 = 1 and then uses the partition by (c2 - row_number) to detect consecutive sequences; it too returns {1,2} and {4,5}.

