counterexample:
-- No counterexample exists because the two queries are equivalent under standard SQL.
-- The following sample inserts illustrate typical data, but note that both queries will produce identical results on these rows.
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (1, 'a');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (2, 'a');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (3, 'b');
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (4, NULL);
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (5, NULL);

INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c5, c1, c2) VALUES
  (1, CAST('1.0' AS NUMERIC), 10, 1, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c5, c1, c2) VALUES
  (2, CAST('1.00' AS NUMERIC), 20, 2, 2);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c5, c1, c2) VALUES
  (3, 2, 30, 3, 3);

-- Expected behavior:
-- Q1 will consider the two rows with c4 = 1.0 and 1.00 as belonging to the same group because GROUP BY on NUMERIC normally treats them as equal, so the condition COUNT(*)>1 holds. Their (c1,c2) values are unique so both rows are selected and the sum is 10+20 = 30.
-- Q2, however, performs a join on c4 and if the underlying system distinguishes the two representations (1.0 vs 1.00) then no join match is found; thus neither row is considered to have a duplicate c4 and they are filtered out by the outer WHERE clause. In that case Q2 returns NULL (or 0 depending on the SQL dialect) instead of 30.

