User 1 installs on 2020-01-01 and logs in twice on 2020-01-02. 
  • Q1’s join will produce three rows for user 1; the CASE expression will add 1 for each of the two 2020-01-02 events so that user 1 contributes a total of 2 in the numerator while the denominator uses COUNT(DISTINCT c4) (so counts user 1 only once).
User 2 installs on 2020-01-01 and logs in once on 2020-01-02.
Thus, for install date '2020-01-01', Q1 computes retention = (2 + 1) / 2 = 1.5, whereas Q2 – depending on whether it might internally handle the duplicates differently – could give a different result if additional deduplication is applied in the join step.

