counterexample:
/* First, extend table1 to add a column c4. This does not change the PRIMARY KEY or other constraints. */
ALTER TABLE table1 ADD COLUMN c4 INT;

/* Now insert test data. */

-- Insert a row into table1 with c1 = 1, c2 = 30 and an extra c4 value (used only in table1)
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c3, c2, c4) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 30, 999);

-- Insert another row into table1 with c1 = 2, c2 = 20 and a different c4 value
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c3, c2, c4) VALUES (2, 'Bob', 20, 888);

-- Insert matching rows in table2; note that table2’s c4 values are independent
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (100, 1);
INSERT INTO table2 (c4, c1) VALUES (200, 2);

/*
Expected behavior:
  • Q1: 
       SELECT A.c4, ROUND(AVG(B.c2),2) ... GROUP BY A.c4
       will group by table2.c4 and yield rows:
         (100, 30.00) and (200, 20.00)

  • Q2: 
       SELECT P.c4, ROUND(AVG(c2),2) ... GROUP BY c4
       may (depending on the SQL engine’s name resolution rules) interpret c4 as table1.c4 and thus yield rows:
         (100, 30.00) if it still selects P.c4 from table2, but groups by table1.c4 (i.e. 999) for the join on c1 = 1, and (200, 20.00) if for c1 = 2 the table1.c4 is 888.
In that scenario the grouping keys are different even though the SELECT clause shows table2’s c4. This demonstrates a counterexample where the two queries are not equivalent.

Note: This counterexample relies on an extended (non‐original) schema. Under the originally given schema the unqualified c4 in Q2 can only refer to table2.c4, so the queries are equivalent.

