counterexample:
-- There is no counterexample under the given schema that makes Q1 and Q2 produce different results.
-- For example, consider the following INSERTs which many might try:
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (1, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (2, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (4, 1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c2, c1) VALUES (5, 1);

-- Q1 will return 1, 2, 4, and 5 because:
-- • Row 1 qualifies because it has row 2 as its right neighbor (2 = 1+1) with c1 = 1.
-- • Row 2 qualifies because it has row 1 as its left neighbor (1 = 2-1) with c1 = 1.
-- • Row 4 qualifies because it has row 5 as its right neighbor (5 = 4+1) with c1 = 1.
-- • Row 5 qualifies because it has row 4 as its left neighbor (4 = 5-1) with c1 = 1.

-- Q2 filters to active rows (rows with c1 <> 0), orders them by c2, and groups them by (c2 - row_number) so that rows 1 and 2 have the same group and rows 4 and 5 have the same group. Since both groups have count >= 2, it returns the same set: 1, 2, 4, and 5.

-- Thus, these INSERTs (or any others obeying the schema) do not yield a difference between Q1 and Q2; indeed, under the stipulated conditions no counterexample exists.

INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'S8', 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (2, 'IPHONE', 200);

