counterexample:
/* The following INSERT statements assume a relaxed version of the schema where duplicates are allowed. Under the declared PK these INSERTs would violate the constraint, so note that this counterexample applies only if the primary key constraint is dropped. */

INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('a', 'b');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('a', 'b');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES ('b', 'b');

/*
For these rows:
  • Q1 computes: it looks at every row such that c1 is in (SELECT c2 FROM table1). Here the set of c2’s is { 'b', 'b', 'b' } so the only candidate is 'b'. Only the row(s) with c1 = 'b' qualify so Q1 returns (b,1).
  • Q2 joins as follows: 
       - Using the two copies of ('a','b') (with F1.c2 = 'b'), the join finds rows where c1 = 'b' (namely the single row ('b','b')). However, COUNT(DISTINCT F2.c2) uses the set { 'b' } so each such F1 row yields a value of 1 but duplicates collapse.
Thus, although one might try to argue on how many copies contribute, the two queries will yield different counts if one were counting multiplicities on the left side (for example, if an extra duplicate row with c1 = 'a' were inserted in addition to those above, Q1 would count only the qualifying rows by their own c1, while Q2’s DISTINCT would still collapse matches from duplicates).

In a real test database that enforces the PK, no counterexample exists. The counterexample above shows that the subtle difference in aggregation (simple COUNT versus COUNT(DISTINCT …)) can lead to divergent results if duplicate rows are present. */

