counterexample:
-- The following INSERT statements illustrate a counterexample in a hypothetical scenario where the primary key on table2 is not enforced (i.e. duplicates are allowed).

-- Inserting into table1 a customer that purchased item 1
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2) VALUES (1, 1);

-- Inserting duplicate rows into table2 (this violates the primary key constraint, but serves as a counterexample in a relaxed schema)
INSERT INTO table2 (c2) VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO table2 (c2) VALUES (1);

/*
Explanation:
  • In table2, COUNT(DISTINCT c2) returns 1, while COUNT(*) returns 2.
  • In table1, customer 1 has purchased one distinct item (1), so Q1’s HAVING condition is satisfied (1 = 1), while Q2’s condition fails (1 <> 2).
  • Thus Q1 returns customer 1, but Q2 does not.

NOTE: Under the declared schema (with table2.c2 as PRIMARY KEY) it is impossible to insert duplicate rows. Therefore, if all constraints are enforced the two queries are equivalent.
*/

-- Under the declared schema a genuine counterexample cannot be produced without violating the primary key constraint. 
-- For demonstration only (this violates the PK constraint):
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 10, 100);
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 20, 200);
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 30, 300);

-- Explanation: In a setting where duplicates for (c4, c2) were allowed, group 1 would have two rows on '2023-01-02'.
-- Thus, Q1 would compute a sum of 2 for that group (since the join would return two rows where c2 = MIN(c2)+1) while Q2 would count this group only once, resulting in different fractions.

-- Since the PRIMARY KEY precludes such duplicate dates for the same c4, no valid counterexample exists under the given schema.

-- Note: The following INSERT statements create a test dataset that demonstrates a difference between Q1 and Q2 only if duplicate logins on the same day are allowed. 
-- Under the current schema (with PRIMARY KEY (c4, c2)) such duplicate entries are forbidden.

-- For the purpose of illustrating the difference, assume that the uniqueness constraint on (c4, c2) is disabled.

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 100, 200);
-- For user 1, two logins on the day after the first login
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 101, 201);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 102, 202);

-- For user 2, only one login on the day after the first login
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 110, 210);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-02', 111, 211);

INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'S8', 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (2, 'IPHONE', 200);

INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 10, 1, '2023-01-01', 0, 0);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 20, NULL, '2023-01-02', 0, 0);

INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'S8', 100);
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (2, 'IPHONE', 200);

-- Insert a row that will be selected on the S8 side with a non-NULL c1
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 0, 10, '2020-01-01', 0, 0);

-- Insert a row for the IPHONE side that has a matching table1 row but a NULL c1
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 0, NULL, '2020-01-02', 0, 0);

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 100, 10);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 101, 11);

INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2023-01-01', 102, 12);

