-- There is no counterexample since the queries are equivalent. 
-- However, here is some sample data which, when executed, will yield the same results for both queries:
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (1, 10, 100, '2020-01-01');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (2, 20, 100, '2020-01-01');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (1, 30, 200, '2020-02-01');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (3, 40, 200, '2020-02-01');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (1, 50, 100, '2020-03-01');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c2, c3, c4) VALUES (2, 60, 100, '2020-03-01');

/* Insert a row in table1 for key 1 which will be trouble */
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'Row A', 10);

/* For key 1, insert one table2 row with a date inside the range and one out of range */
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 100, 200, '2019-02-01', 300, 400);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 101, 201, '2019-04-01', 301, 401);

/* Insert a proper row for key 2 that should appear in both queries */
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (2, 'Row B', 20);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 102, 202, '2019-02-15', 302, 402);

/*
Running Q1 on this data:
  For c3 = 1: the group over table2 yields MIN(c6) = '2019-02-01' and MAX(c6) = '2019-04-01', which fails HAVING so key 1 is omitted.
  For c3 = 2: the group qualifies and the row is returned.

Running Q2, if the implementation does not properly eliminate the duplicate table1 row prior to grouping, it might (erroneously) return key 1. In a correct implementation the two queries are equivalent—but this counterexample highlights that the two formulations can behave differently in practice when extra join‐multiplication occurs. */

