counterexample:
-- The following INSERT statements create test data that illustrates the difference if multiple login events on the same day were allowed (i.e. if the PK constraint were relaxed):
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-01', 10, 100);
-- User 1 logs in twice on the day after installation (day+1)
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 11, 101);
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2020-01-02', 12, 102);

-- User 2 installs on the same day but does not return on day+1
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-01', 20, 200);
-- User 2 has a later login which does not count as day+1
INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2020-01-03', 21, 201);

/*
Under these data (assuming duplicate day records were permitted),
• Q1’s inner join will produce for install date 2020-01-01 a denominator of 2 (users 1 and 2) but a numerator of 2 (user 1’s two logins on 2020-01-02) - yielding DAY1_RETENTION = ROUND(2/2,2) = 1.00.
• Q2’s LEFT JOIN with COUNT(DISTINCT) will count user 1 only once for a day+1 login, so the numerator will be 1, yielding DAY1_RETENTION = ROUND(1/2,2) = 0.50.
*/

