/*
Explanation of the counterexample:
  • For user 1, the subquery (or CTE) returns the install date of '2020-01-01'.
  • However, in Q2 the join to table1 yields two rows for user 1 with c2 = '2020-01-01' (because of the duplicate), so the conditional SUM counts 2 installs for that date, while Q1’s COUNT(DISTINCT c4) counts only 1.
  • This causes the DAY1_RETENTION ratio (day+1 logins divided by installs) in Q2 to be computed as 1/2 = 0.50 for the install date, while Q1 computes it as 1/1 = 1.00.
Note: Under the declared schema this counterexample violates the PRIMARY KEY constraint. It illustrates the only scenario in which the two queries would diverge. If the PK constraint is enforced, no valid counterexample exists.
*/

